<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etemenanki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Etemenanki, 'temple of the foundation of heaven and earth', was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, standing beside his temple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>. Rebuilt on a vast scale by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, it rose in stepped stages over a base about 91 metres square. It is widely thought to have inspired the biblical story of the Tower of Babel and was dismantled after the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etemenanki.jpg" alt="Etemenanki" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etemenanki_drawing.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>It is unclear when Etemenanki was first built. Andrew R. George argues that it dates from between 1500 and 1000 BCE, because a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian</a> copy of the Creation Epic, Enûma Eliš, already refers to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Other scholars see a longer history of successive construction, destruction and reconstruction reaching back to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. Sennacherib destroyed Babylon in 689 BCE and claimed to have destroyed the Etemenanki. Restoration of the city took 88 years, begun by the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> and continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, who rebuilt the ziggurat beside the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> dated to 229 BCE, a copy of an older text known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a> tablet, describes the tower as seven stages high with a square base 91 metres on each side. Robert Koldewey's excavations after 1913 confirmed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> structure, with large stairs on the south side where a triple gate connected it with Esagila, and a larger gate on the east leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/processional-way">processional way</a>. Modern scholars dispute the stated height of 91 metres; Fenollós and colleagues propose about 54 metres, or 66 with the temple at the top.</p><p>A stele in the Schøyen Collection, identified in 2003 as the oldest known depiction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, dates to 604 to 562 BCE. It shows the high first stages with paired flights of steps, five further stepped stages and the temple on top. Foundation cylinders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a>, found in the 1880s, record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> told him the ziggurat was badly buckled and ordered him to found it on the breast of the netherworld and make its top vie with the heavens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described a tower of eight stages in the 5th century BCE, but his account is probably second hand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 331 BCE he ordered repairs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, but he found no progress when he returned in 323 BCE, and his death prevented the work. Later chronicles record several attempts to rebuild it, each preceded by clearing the last debris. The Seleucid crown prince Antiochus I made a sacrifice in preparation, stumbled on the rubble, and angrily ordered his elephant drivers to destroy the last remains. No later references to the Etemenanki survive from antiquity. Some scholars propose it as the ziggurat that may have influenced the biblical account of the Tower of Babel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Etemenanki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ishtar Gate, the Processional Way, the rebuilt temple of Marduk with its ziggurat <b>Etemenanki</b>, the South Palace and the Hanging Gardens, whose existence is still debated, all show a state putting surplus revenue into monumental architecture with deliberate ideological intent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
